INT. TEA HOUSE - 1893 - AFTERNOON

Lacquered screens, a low table, steam curling from a cast-iron kettle. Rain taps the paper windows.

LADY BEATRIX HALLORAN-SATO (40s, silk kimono over a Western corset) pours tea with deliberate grace. Across from her, MISS PHILIPPA ROOK (30s, travelling suit, gloves in her lap) watches the cups. Beside her, DR. EMERIC CAULFIELD (50s, wing collar) turns his spoon. Two servants kneel by the door: TAKEO (20s, precise) and OTOMO (20s, hands folded).

She pours Miss Rook's cup last. Miss Rook accepts it. Neither speaks.

DR. CAULFIELD
A fine blend.

LADY BEATRIX
My late husband's.

Miss Rook lifts the cup. Lady Beatrix watches her lips touch the rim. Takeo's eyes dart to Otomo. Otomo's hands tighten.

MISS ROOK
It has a faint bitterness.

LADY BEATRIX
So does his memory.

Miss Rook sets the cup down. She smooths her gloves. Dr. Caulfield sips and sets his cup to one side.

DR. CAULFIELD
I'm sure it is nothing.

Miss Rook rises. She crosses to the window and slides it open an inch. Rain mists her face. Lady Beatrix does not turn, but her shoulders loosen by a fraction. The kettle sings. Otomo bows low and does not rise.
